# MoE OTD: drop the unconditional stream.finish() before on_before_batched_gemv's topk read
#
# on_before_batched_gemv (src/graph/impls/ocl_v2/moe/moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp,
# offloaded path) opened with an unconditional stream.finish() - a full queue
# drain, once per MoE layer, once per token (x40 layers). The read it
# protects is the very next line, a blocking scratch.topk_id->copy_to(...,
# blocking=true), which reads back the routing kernel's output enqueued a few
# lines earlier on the same stream. On an in_order queue that blocking
# copy_to already waits for every kernel enqueued before it in program order
# - the routing kernel included - so the drain was redundant there and only
# cost time; the design named it worth measuring only after patches/0005 (P1)
# landed, since P1 is what removes the per-token PCIe stream that otherwise
# dominates and would have hidden this. Gated the same way patches/0006 (P2)
# gates its own async path: stream.get_queue_type() == QueueTypes::in_order
# skips the drain (relying on the copy_to's own blocking wait for
# correctness); any other queue type keeps the original unconditional
# stream.finish(), since program order gives no such guarantee there and this
# patch does not try to prove out-of-order correctness. Applied against the
# pinned runtime, build 2026.4.0-22849-71640275d29, on top of patches/0003-
# 0006.
#
# Scope: only the site the design names (moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp:736, inside
# on_before_batched_gemv). Two other stream.finish() calls nearby
# (exec_prefill_grouped_gemm's "ensure routing kernel has written topk_ids"
# drain, and on_after_exec_sync/on_before_grouped_gather's drains) are left
# untouched - they were not what the design's A/B names, and each guards a
# different read with its own reasoning that this patch has not re-derived.
#
# Its own A/B per docs/design-m9-offload-v2.md §1's P2b line
# ("worth [to measure] only after P1"): not run here - no GPU access from
# this task. The expected observable
# is per-token host time falling by the removed drains, unchanged output
# otherwise; a regression there (wrong output, or no measurable change) sends
# this patch back per the design's own gate.
#
# REGRESSION, measured on-card with patches/0006 also applied: u8-KV serving
# produces deterministic garbage under the patched plugin (vanilla plugin
# coherent, same everything else; reproduces at pool cap 0, so patches/0005
# is not implicated); u4-KV through the same patched plugin is coherent.
# Leading theory: a timing-exposed race, u8 and u4 PagedAttention kernels
# running at different speeds and shifting which interleaving of async work
# this drain's removal exposes. This invalidates the cycle-2 sweep numbers
# taken under this patch until resolved (fast wrong compute) - flagged on
# the campaign record, not just here.
#
# Kill-switch for on-card isolation: MOE_OTD_FORCE_FINISH=1 restores the
# unconditional stream.finish() this patch gated, regardless of queue type -
# i.e. this build behaves as if this patch were not applied, while 0006
# stays in effect (independently switchable via its own MOE_OTD_FORCE_
# SYNC_UPLOAD=1 - see that patch's header for the 2x2 the two switches
# form). Read once, the same way MOE_OTD_PERF_LOG is read; default off,
# unchanged from this patch as landed.
#
# Re-audit, with the race lens - this is where confidence drops the most:
# the argument above proves only that the blocking copy_to protects the
# *topk_id* read from the routing kernel that produced it. It says nothing
# about a second role the removed drain plausibly played: on the *previous*
# token's iteration through this same function, the batched-GEMV kernel
# enqueued at the end of that iteration reads the expert slot buffers this
# iteration's fill_weights_memory (inside try_acquire_simultaneous, called a
# few lines below the drain this patch touches) is about to overwrite for
# any expert that got evicted and reassigned. In-order execution on the one
# cl::CommandQueue the stream owns (verified: ocl_common.hpp, one
# cl::CommandQueue per ocl_stream, no separate compute/copy queue) should
# still serialize that correctly without stream.finish() - GPU-side
# execution order follows enqueue order regardless of blocking vs non-
# blocking submission, and token N's exec_batched_gemv() is not called
# until token N-1's has returned, so the host issues N-1's kernel enqueue
# before N's copy enqueue. That chain was not verified end to end against
# the live serving loop (only read from this file's code), and the
# measurement says something in it does not hold - most likely either an
# assumption above is wrong, or the actual fault is on 0006's side (a
# staging-ring reuse or event-wait detail; see that patch's header), or an
# interaction between the two that neither patch's argument, read alone,
# would surface. Nothing here is confirmed broken and nothing is retracted;
# the switches above are what turns this from an argument into a
# measurement.
diff --git a/src/plugins/intel_gpu/src/graph/impls/ocl_v2/moe/moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp b/src/plugins/intel_gpu/src/graph/impls/ocl_v2/moe/moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp
--- a/src/plugins/intel_gpu/src/graph/impls/ocl_v2/moe/moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp
+++ b/src/plugins/intel_gpu/src/graph/impls/ocl_v2/moe/moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp
@@ -2,6 +2,8 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 //
 
+#include <cstdlib>
+
 // clang-format off
 #include "moe_3gemm_gen_micro.hpp"
 #include "moe_otd_runtime.hpp"
@@ -733,7 +735,32 @@
             needs_fallback = false;
             return;
         }
-        stream.finish();
+        // arcint M9 (patches/0007, P2b). This used to be an unconditional
+        // stream.finish() — a full queue drain every MoE layer, every token.
+        // The read it protects is the blocking copy_to right below, which
+        // reads scratch.topk_id back from the routing kernel that produced
+        // it a few lines earlier on this same stream. On an in_order queue
+        // that copy_to(..., blocking=true) already waits for every kernel
+        // enqueued before it in program order, topk_id's producer included,
+        // so the drain is redundant there and only costs time. Kept as a
+        // fallback for an out_of_order queue (never exercised on our cards:
+        // execution_config.cpp:344 forces in_order whenever use_onednn is
+        // set, true for both), where program order gives no such guarantee.
+        //
+        // arcint M9 review round: a measured u8-KV regression (deterministic
+        // garbage, reproduces at pool cap 0 so it is not 0005) narrows to
+        // 0006 or this patch; the correctness argument above only reasons
+        // about the topk_id read, not about whether the *previous*
+        // iteration's GEMV kernel (which reads the same expert slot buffers
+        // this iteration's fill_weights_memory is about to overwrite) is
+        // guaranteed complete without the drain - see this patch's header
+        // for the re-audit. MOE_OTD_FORCE_FINISH=1 restores the drain
+        // unconditionally, the same way MOE_OTD_PERF_LOG is read, to let
+        // the two hypotheses (this patch vs 0006) be isolated on-card.
+        // Default off: behaviour is unchanged from patches/0007 as landed.
+        static const bool force_finish = std::getenv("MOE_OTD_FORCE_FINISH") != nullptr;
+        if (force_finish || stream.get_queue_type() != cldnn::QueueTypes::in_order)
+            stream.finish();
 
         std::vector<uint32_t> expert_ids(topk_count);
         scratch.topk_id->copy_to(stream, expert_ids.data(), 0, 0, topk_count * sizeof(uint32_t), true);
